%S A402613 0,2,4,8,13,19,25,32,38,47,57,67,78,91,104,122,136,153,168,187,205,
%T A402613 227,249,272,295,320,344,370,402,428,456,485,513,543,575,604,639,673,
%U A402613 706,744,781,818,858,896,936,977,1018,1058,1098,1142,1187,1230,1278,1324
%N A402613 Sum of the entries in the n-th row of A032531.
%C A402613 By definition, this sequence is nonnegative and infinite, by counting it is possible to prove that it is strictly increasing, and diverges to infinity as A401242 does. The asymptotic growth rate of this sequence is unknown, though it has an upper bound of n*(n+3)/2, so it grows at least linearly and at most quadratically. The first 10000 terms depict approximately quadratic growth due to the triangular nature of A032531.
%H A402613 Yifeng Zou, <a href="/A402613/b402613.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%e A402613 For n=3, a(3)=8, as row 3 of A032531 consists of 2,3,1,2, which sums to 8.
%o A402613 (Python)
%o A402613 from collections import Counter
%o A402613 def row_sums_A032531(n):
%o A402613     counts = Counter()
%o A402613     result = []
%o A402613     for row in range(n + 1):
%o A402613         total = 0
%o A402613         for k in range(row + 1):
%o A402613             value = counts[k]
%o A402613             total += value
%o A402613             counts[value] += 1
%o A402613         result.append(total)
%o A402613     return result
%o A402613 print(row_sums_A032531(53))
%Y A402613 Cf. A032531, A401242.
%K A402613 nonn,new
%O A402613 0,2
%A A402613 _Yifeng Zou_, Nov 21 2026
